          "supplier": "def-qc-quantum-channel",
          "relation": "direct",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "Let $H$ and $K$ be finite-dimensional (have finite bases by [[def-dimension]])\ncomplex inner-product spaces. A\n**quantum channel** from $H$ to $K$ is a complex-linear map\n$\\Phi:\\mathcal L(H)\\to\\mathcal L(K)$ ([[def-linear-map]]) that is completely\npositive ([[def-qc-completely-positive-map]]) and trace-preserving, meaning\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(\\Phi(X))=\\operatorname{tr}(X)\\qquad\\text{for every }X\\in\\mathcal L(H),$$\n\nwhere trace is the basis-independent trace of a finite-dimensional\nendomorphism ([[def-trace-of-an-endomorphism]]). Trace preservation is\nrequired on the full operator space, not only on density operators. This\ndefinition is mathematical and assumes no physical realization.",
          "readiness": "published"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qc-density-operator",
          "relation": "direct",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "Let $H$ be a nonzero finite-dimensional complex inner-product space, with the root library's convention that the inner product is linear in its first argument. A **density operator** on $H$ is a non-negative endomorphism $\\rho:H\\to H$ with $\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho)=1$; non-negative and trace have the meanings of [[def-non-negative-and-positive-operator]] and [[def-trace-of-an-endomorphism]]. The zero-dimensional space is excluded: its only endomorphism has trace $0$.\n\nHere $\\ker\\rho=\\{x\\in H:\\rho x=0\\}$, using the kernel of a linear map from [[def-kernel-and-image-of-a-linear-map]]. The **support** of $\\rho$ is the orthogonal complement of its kernel in the root convention:\n\n$$\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho):=\\{x\\in H: \\langle x,y\\rangle=0\\text{ for every }y\\in\\ker\\rho\\}.$$\n\nFor a subspace $S\\subseteq H$, $\\rho$ is **faithful on $S$** when $\\operatorname{supp}(\\rho)=S$ and the restriction $\\rho|_S:S\\to S$ is positive definite in the sense of [[def-non-negative-and-positive-operator]]. This restriction is an endomorphism: if $x\\in S$ and $y\\in\\ker\\rho$, self-adjointness gives $\\langle\\rho x,y\\rangle=\\langle x,\\rho y\\rangle=0$, so $\\rho x\\in(\\ker\\rho)^\\perp=S$.",
          "readiness": "published"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qc-partial-trace",
          "relation": "direct",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "For a complex vector space $H$, write $\\operatorname{End}(H)$ for the set of\nlinear maps $H\\to H$ ([[def-linear-map]]).\n\nLet $H_A,H_B$ be finite-dimensional complex inner-product spaces with fixed\nordered orthonormal bases $(e_i)_{i<a}$ and $(f_j)_{j<b}$, respectively. The\nordered product basis of $H_A\\otimes H_B$ has the $A$ index first. For\n$R\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A\\otimes H_B)$ define\n$\\operatorname{Tr}_B(R)\\in\\operatorname{End}(H_A)$ by\n\n$$\\bigl(\\operatorname{Tr}_B(R)\\bigr)_{i i'}:=\\sum_{j<b}R_{(i,j),(i',j)}.$$\n\nRows are output coordinates and columns are input coordinates, as in\n[[def-coordinate-column-and-matrix-of-a-linear-map]]. If $b=0$, the sum is\nempty and equals $0$. The notation $\\operatorname{Tr}_B$ means that the second\nfactor is contracted; the first factor and its order are retained.",
          "readiness": "published"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-adjoint-of-a-linear-map-between-inner-product-spaces",
          "relation": "direct",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "Let $V,W$ be inner product spaces ([[def-inner-product-space]]) over the same field and let $T:V\\to W$ be linear ([[def-linear-map]]). An **adjoint** of $T$ is a linear map $T^*:W\\to V$ such that\n\n$$\\langle Tv,w\\rangle_W=\\langle v,T^*w\\rangle_V$$\n\nfor every $v\\in V$ and $w\\in W$. Existence is not part of the definition; in finite dimensions it will follow from Riesz representation.",
          "readiness": "published"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-qc-tensor-register-and-permutation",
          "relation": "direct",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "excerpt": "An **ordered tensor register** consists of a finite ordered list of\nfinite-dimensional complex inner-product spaces $H_0,\\ldots,H_{n-1}$, each\nequipped with a specified ordered orthonormal basis. Its space is\n$H_0\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes H_{n-1}$, with factors retained in the displayed\norder. The product basis is ordered lexicographically by its tuple of factor\nindices. The empty tensor product is $\\mathbb C$ with basis $\\{1\\}$. A\nzero-dimensional factor is allowed in this mathematical construction and makes\nthe tensor space zero; it is not a register under\n[[def-qc-register-and-computational-basis]].\n\nThe inner product is linear in the first argument and conjugate-linear in the\nsecond, and on elementary tensors it is\n\n$$\\left\\langle\\bigotimes_{k<n}x_k,\\bigotimes_{k<n}y_k\\right\\rangle_{\\otimes}:=\\prod_{k<n}\\langle x_k,y_k\\rangle_{{\\rm root},k}.$$ \n\nFor the empty tensor, the right side is the empty product $1$, giving the\nstandard inner product on $\\mathbb C$.\n\nFor a permutation $\\sigma$ of the $n$ factor positions, the **wire permutation**\n$P_\\sigma$ maps the product basis vector indexed by\n$(i_0,\\ldots,i_{n-1})$ to the product basis vector whose output factor order is\n$(\\sigma(0),\\ldots,\\sigma(n-1))$. It is extended linearly; for $n=0$ it is the\nidentity on $\\mathbb C$. The extension is a linear map in the sense of\n[[def-linear-map]].",
          "readiness": "published"
        },
        {
          "supplier": "cor-dimension-of-a-direct-sum",
          "relation": "direct",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "excerpt": "Let $F$ be a field ([[def-field]]), let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, let $V$ be a vector\nspace over $F$ ([[def-vector-space]]) and let $U$ be a family of linear subspaces\n$U_i$ of $V$ indexed by $i < n$ ([[def-linear-subspace]],\n[[def-sum-of-linear-subspaces]]) with\n\n$$V \\;=\\; \\bigoplus_{i<n} U_i$$\n\n([[def-internal-direct-sum]]) and every $U_i$ finite-dimensional over $F$\n([[def-dimension]]). Then $V$ is finite-dimensional over $F$ and\n\n$$\\dim_F V \\;=\\; \\sum_{i<n} \\dim_F U_i ,$$\n\nthe right-hand side being the finite sum of [[def-monoid-finite-product]] read\nadditively in the commutative monoid $(\\mathbb{N}, +, 0)$\n([[def-nat-addition]], [[lem-nat-add-identity]],\n[[lem-nat-add-associative]], [[lem-nat-add-commutative]]).\n\n**The base case is a genuine case.** At $n = 0$ the direct sum of the empty\nfamily is $\\{0_V\\}$ and the empty sum of natural numbers is $0$, so the formula\nreads $\\dim_F\\{0_V\\} = 0$. **At $n = 2$** it reads\n$\\dim_F(U \\oplus W) = \\dim_F U + \\dim_F W$.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The only inputs are\n[[thm-dimension-formula]] and [[thm-dimension-of-a-linear-subspace]], both of\nwhich are proved in finite dimension without one.",
          "readiness": "published"
        },
