          "claim": "For any input state $\\rho$ and positive integer $m$, exact spectral type reduction gives a flat state $\\tau_t$ on an $m$-use input subspace such that $$f_{\\mathcal N^{\\otimes m}}(\\tau_t)\\ge m f_{\\mathcal N}(\\rho)-2d\\ln(m+1),\\qquad d=\\dim A.$$ Apply the flat-state construction to that fixed block channel. For fixed $m$, both its Weyl alphabet and its receiver output dimension are finite, so the HSW theorem applies before any limit in $m$ is taken. The shared resource is the flat type-subspace maximally entangled state for each outer block. Padding fewer than $m$ unused original-channel inputs with a fixed state gives codes for all sufficiently large lengths and no asymptotic rate loss. Therefore $$C_E(\\mathcal N)\\ge f_{\\mathcal N}(\\rho)-\\frac{2d\\ln(m+1)}m.$$ Since $\\ln(m+1)/m\\to0$ by [F13], letting $m\\to\\infty$ and maximizing over $\\rho$ proves the general direct bound with precisely the stated unlimited finite-shared-entanglement model. No unrestricted quantum-input HSW single-letter theorem and no inference from Schumacher compression alone is used.",
