        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-qc-schmidt-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $H,K$ be finite-dimensional complex-inner-product spaces with the tensor-product convention of [[def-qc-tensor-register-and-permutation]]. For every $\\Psi\\in H\\otimes K$ there are an integer $r\\ge0$, positive numbers $s_1,\\ldots,s_r$, and orthonormal families $u_1,\\ldots,u_r\\in H$ and $v_1,\\ldots,v_r\\in K$ such that\n\n$$\\Psi=\\sum_{j=1}^r s_j u_j\\otimes v_j,\\qquad \\|\\Psi\\|^2=\\sum_{j=1}^r s_j^2.$$\n\nFor $\\Psi=0$ take $r=0$ and the empty sum. For any chosen ordered pair of orthonormal bases, the construction below obtains the $s_j$ as the positive singular values of the coefficient matrix of $\\Psi$ in their ordered product basis.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-trace-of-an-endomorphism",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional vector space over $F$ and let $T:V\\to V$ be\nlinear. Choose any ordered basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V$ and define the **trace of\nthe endomorphism $T$** by\n\n$$\\operatorname{tr}(T):=\\operatorname{tr}([T]_{\\mathcal B}^{\\mathcal B}).$$\n\nThis is independent of the chosen basis: matrices of the same endomorphism in\ntwo ordered bases are similar, and similar matrices have equal trace. When\n$V$ is the zero space, the empty basis gives trace $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-non-negative-operator-characterisations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real or complex inner product space and let\n$T:V\\to V$ be linear. The following are equivalent:\n\n1. $T$ is non-negative.\n2. $T$ is self-adjoint and every eigenvalue of $T$ is a nonnegative real number.\n3. Some orthonormal basis makes the matrix of $T$ diagonal with nonnegative real diagonal entries.\n4. There exists a linear map $S:V\\to V$ such that $T=S^*S$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-it-finite-dimensional-quantum-relative-entropy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $H$ be a finite-dimensional complex inner-product space and let $A,B:H\\to H$ be non-negative operators. For such an operator $A$, set $\\operatorname{supp}(A):=(\\ker A)^\\perp$, where $\\ker A$ is the kernel of a linear map ([[def-kernel-and-image-of-a-linear-map]]); this extends the density-operator support convention in [[def-qc-density-operator]]. If $\\operatorname{supp}(A)\\subseteq\\operatorname{supp}(B)$, define\n\n$$D(A\\|B):=\\operatorname{tr}(A\\ln A)-\\operatorname{tr}(A\\ln B).$$\n\nHere $\\ln A$ is defined spectrally on the positive eigenspaces of $A$ with $0\\ln0:=0$, and $\\ln B$ in the cross term is restricted to $\\operatorname{supp}(B)$; the support condition ensures that $A$ vanishes on $\\ker B$, so the omitted kernel contributes zero. If $\\operatorname{supp}(A)\\nsubseteq\\operatorname{supp}(B)$, set $D(A\\|B):=+\\infty$. In particular, $D(0\\|B)=0$. For density operators this is the finite-dimensional **quantum relative entropy**. The logarithms are natural, so the value is in nats; the base-two value is $D_2(A\\|B):=D(A\\|B)/\\ln2$.",
          "uses": [
